    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="0318" n="220" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg220"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11 Changsha/10: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-12-09T17:00:00-05:00">December 9, 1930—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>424. Your 1025 of December 2, 4 p.m.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d271-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>If in your further discussion of the Changsha incident with the Chinese
                        authorities it should appear that there is a possibility that such claims
                        may be treated as were the Nanking claims, you may state that the Department
                        would be willing to accept a similar arrangement for the adjudication of the
                        Changsha claims.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Please keep Department informed promptly with regard to developments and
                        any steps that are being or may be taken by other foreign
                        governments.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
